---
// src/components/AchievementIslandSection.astro
//
// Same family as ComputerIslandSection.astro: two flanking
// illustrations sitting behind centered-ish text, one JS-driven
// mouse-repel script, one CSS bob loop per island. Neither island
// bleeds off the section's edges here either, so positioning is plain
// vw/vh (same reasoning as ComputerIslandSection.astro — a vw/vh
// offset already scales continuously with the viewport, so there's no
// need for the clamp(mobileValue, calc(a + b*vw), desktopValue) trick
// ArtIslandSection.astro needs for its bleeding corners).
//
// Per your reference screenshot, the arrangement is close to
// ComputerIslandSection's but tuned differently:
//
//   - `leftImage`  (statue island, svg) -> mid-left, narrower and
//     taller than the telephone-pole island was, sitting a little
//     further in from the edge.
//   - `rightImage` (temple island, svg) -> right side, large, sitting
//     lower and tucked in much closer to the right edge than the
//     computer island was.
//
// (The mountain shape bottom-left in your screenshot is the
// persistent CornerIsland.astro front island that's on every section
// via Layout.astro — not one of this component's two images.)
//
// Included a mobile media query mirroring ComputerIslandSection's:
// left island drops into normal flow below the text (via `order`)
// instead of overlaying it, right island gets a tighter `right` so it
// doesn't creep under the now full-width text. Same as the other
// sections in this family, treat these as starting values to nudge
// once you see it live.
//
// Background: real image (`backgroundImage`), via `background-image`
// + `background-size: cover`, `min-height: 100vh` — same technique
// every section in this family uses. Every section keeps a CONSISTENT
// height across every screen size, with the background's left/right
// edges cropped as needed to make that happen.
//
// `background-position: top` protects the true top edge — the one
// that has to match EducationIslandSection's bottom — from ever being
// cropped. The bottom edge (against BonusIslandSection) is the
// lower-priority side here.
//
// Carries `seam-fade-bottom` (blending into var(--dusk-blue)) against
// BonusIslandSection's top. Pixel-sampling your screenshots showed
// this edge's actual color isn't fixed — it's an arbitrary crop of
// interior content, not a real matched edge — so this fade is a
// deliberate mask rather than a precision fix (see the shared comment
// in Layout.astro).
//
// Also carries `seam-fade-top` (blending into var(--sky-3)) against
// EducationIslandSection's bottom. That transition looked fine on the
// screens tested earlier, but broke too on some wide/short viewports
// — same fade treatment applied here now for consistency.
//
// Still carries `.sky-chain`, so the gap-collapsing on both sides
// keeps working regardless of what backs it.
//
// Carries `.halftone-hover`, joining the single, page-wide cursor-
// spotlight element in Layout.astro (`.halftone-spotlight`, see the
// comment there) purely by having this class — no markup or script
// needed here for it, since `body:has(.halftone-hover:hover)
// .halftone-spotlight` is what actually turns the spotlight on.
//
// New: two optional "front chunk" images — `rightChunk1Image` /
// `rightChunk2Image` (your right_achievement_island_first_front_
// chunk.webp / right_achievement_island_second_front_chunk.webp) —
// smaller rock fragments that sit in front of and below the main
// temple island, per your reference screenshot: chunk 1 (the bigger
// one) tucked just under-left of the temple's base, chunk 2 (smaller)
// further down-left, partly overlapping chunk 1. Positioned with the
// same plain vw/vh approach as the two main islands, as starting
// values eyeballed off your screenshot — nudge once it's live, same
// as everything else in this family.
//
// Both chunks get the SAME bob-loop + mouse-repel treatment as
// `leftImage`/`rightImage` (own `.achievement-glitch` outer wrapper
// for the CSS bob animation, `.achievement-glitch-inner.parallax-
// inner` child for the JS drift — the existing script below already
// picks these up for free, since it queries `.parallax-inner`
// globally like every other section in this family). What's new is
// that they're NOT always visible: per your reference (the small
// symbols/pictures that flicker in and out as you move the cursor on
// rossdraws.com), each chunk is normally invisible and only glitches
// into view when the cursor is close to where it sits — moving away
// lets it linger for a couple seconds before it hides again, rather
// than vanishing the instant the cursor leaves, and it keeps
// re-glitching at random intervals for as long as it lingers instead
// of flashing once and then sitting there static. Each glitch plays a
// quick "digital tear" flourish (a banded `clip-path` jump-cut plus a
// brief hue-rotate flash, both using `steps()` easing so the
// transition SNAPS between states instead of smoothly tweening) and a
// scanline texture overlay that's only present mid-flash.
//
// The proximity check is a dedicated `mousemove` listener in the
// script below (separate from the mouse-repel drift's own listener,
// even though both read the same cursor position) — it measures the
// distance from the cursor to each chunk's own static rest position
// (the OUTER `.achievement-glitch` element's `getBoundingClientRect()`,
// which doesn't move even while its inner child is actively drifting,
// since a child's `transform` never affects its parent's layout box)
// and toggles `.is-visible` once that distance crosses
// `revealRadius`. The one-shot glitch-pop/glitch-scan CSS animations
// are restarted on every reveal by removing the `.is-visible` class,
// forcing a reflow (`void chunk.offsetWidth`), then re-adding it —
// without the forced reflow, re-adding a class whose animation never
// really "finished" registering as removed wouldn't reliably restart
// the animation from frame 0 in every browser.
//
// `pointer-events: none` stays on `.achievement-glitch` (same as
// every other island) since these are purely decorative and
// `aria-hidden`, so this can't use native `:hover`/`mouseenter` —
// those require the element to actually receive pointer events. The
// distance-based JS check works around that without needing the
// chunk itself to be interactive.
//
// Both chunks default to `z-index: 1`, same tier as `.achievement-
// island` — this matters for the halftone spotlight in Layout.astro:
// that element sits at `z-index: 0` specifically so everything at
// `z-index: 1`+ paints over it, untouched by its dot-pattern blend.
// Keeping the chunks at that same tier means they're excluded from
// the halftone tint exactly like the other islands are, rather than
// (accidentally) being treated as part of the background.
//
// Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, both chunks are shown
// statically (no glitch animation, no scanline texture, no
// proximity-gating) — popping in and out as the cursor moves is
// exactly the kind of motion that setting is meant to suppress, so
// this shows them plainly instead of just hiding them outright.
//
// On narrow screens (`max-width: 640px`) both chunks are hidden
// entirely rather than repositioned — they're purely decorative
// flourish, not core content, and there isn't a clean spot for them
// once the layout collapses to the mobile single-column treatment.
// Ask if you'd rather they show up (smaller, repositioned) on mobile
// too.
//
// NOTE: I still don't have the actual background/chunk image files
// for this section — this is reasoned from first principles, not
// measured against the real files.
//
// Usage:
//   <AchievementIslandSection
//     id="section-achievements"
//     eyebrow="Island 04"
//     title="Do Eiusmod Tempor"
//     text="Nemo enim ipsam voluptatem quia voluptas sit aspernatur..."
//     leftImage="/images/left_achievement_island.svg"
//     rightImage="/images/right_achievement_island.svg"
//     rightChunk1Image="/images/right_achievement_island_first_front_chunk.webp"
//     rightChunk2Image="/images/right_achievement_island_second_front_chunk.webp"
//     backgroundImage="/images/achievement_island_background.webp"
//   />

interface Props {
  id: string;
  eyebrow?: string;
  title: string;
  text: string;
  leftImage: string;
  rightImage: string;
  rightChunk1Image?: string;
  rightChunk2Image?: string;
  backgroundImage: string;
}

const {
  id,
  eyebrow,
  title,
  text,
  leftImage,
  rightImage,
  rightChunk1Image,
  rightChunk2Image,
  backgroundImage,
} = Astro.props;
---

<section
  class="achievement-section sky-chain seam-fade-top seam-fade-bottom halftone-hover"
  id={id}
  style={`background-image: url(${backgroundImage});`}
>
  <div class="achievement-island achievement-island-left" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="achievement-island-inner parallax-inner">
      <img src={leftImage} alt="" />
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="content">
    {eyebrow && <p class="eyebrow">{eyebrow}</p>}
    <h2>{title}</h2>
    <p class="body-text">{text}</p>
  </div>

  <div class="achievement-island achievement-island-right" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="achievement-island-inner parallax-inner">
      <img src={rightImage} alt="" />
    </div>
  </div>

  {rightChunk1Image && (
    <div class="achievement-glitch achievement-glitch-1" aria-hidden="true">
      <div class="achievement-glitch-inner parallax-inner">
        <img src={rightChunk1Image} alt="" />
      </div>
    </div>
  )}

  {rightChunk2Image && (
    <div class="achievement-glitch achievement-glitch-2" aria-hidden="true">
      <div class="achievement-glitch-inner parallax-inner">
        <img src={rightChunk2Image} alt="" />
      </div>
    </div>
  )}
</section>

<script>
  // Same proximity-repel drift as the rest of this family, driving
  // every `.parallax-inner` element on the page. If more than one of
  // these sections lands on the same page, each ships its own copy of
  // this script and each queries ALL `.parallax-inner` elements
  // globally (not just its own) — intentional/harmless (they all
  // compute the same easing toward the same targets), just a little
  // redundant. Worth hoisting into one shared script if this family
  // keeps growing.
  const islands = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(".parallax-inner");
  const prefersReducedMotion = window.matchMedia(
    "(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)"
  ).matches;

  if (islands.length && !prefersReducedMotion) {
    const maxOffset = 36; // px, strongest push when cursor is right on top of the island
    const influenceRadius = 500; // px, push fades to 0 beyond this distance
    const ease = 0.08;

    let mouseX = 0;
    let mouseY = 0;
    let hasMouse = false;

    const state = Array.from(islands).map((el) => ({
      el,
      currentX: 0,
      currentY: 0,
    }));

    window.addEventListener("mousemove", (e) => {
      mouseX = e.clientX;
      mouseY = e.clientY;
      hasMouse = true;
    });

    function tick() {
      for (const s of state) {
        let targetX = 0;
        let targetY = 0;

        if (hasMouse) {
          const rect = s.el.getBoundingClientRect();
          const restX = rect.left + rect.width / 2 - s.currentX;
          const restY = rect.top + rect.height / 2 - s.currentY;

          const dx = restX - mouseX;
          const dy = restY - mouseY;
          const dist = Math.hypot(dx, dy) || 1;

          if (dist < influenceRadius) {
            const falloff = 1 - dist / influenceRadius;
            const nx = dx / dist;
            const ny = dy / dist;
            targetX = nx * maxOffset * falloff;
            targetY = ny * maxOffset * falloff;
          }
        }

        s.currentX += (targetX - s.currentX) * ease;
        s.currentY += (targetY - s.currentY) * ease;
        s.el.style.transform = `translate(${s.currentX.toFixed(2)}px, ${s.currentY.toFixed(2)}px)`;
      }
      requestAnimationFrame(tick);
    }

    requestAnimationFrame(tick);
  }

  // Cursor-proximity reveal for the two front rock chunks (see
  // `.achievement-glitch` in the <style> below). Each chunk is
  // normally invisible; moving the cursor within `revealRadius` of
  // where it sits glitches it into view, moving back out hides it
  // again. Distance is measured against each chunk's own static rest
  // position — the OUTER `.achievement-glitch` element's
  // `getBoundingClientRect()`, which stays put regardless of the
  // mouse-repel `transform` applied to its INNER `.parallax-inner`
  // child (a transform on a child never affects its parent's layout
  // box), so this stays accurate even while a chunk is actively
  // drifting. This is a separate listener from the mouse-repel one
  // above (even though both read the same cursor position) since the
  // two do fundamentally different things with it: one eases a
  // transform every frame, this one just checks a distance and flips
  // a class.
  const glitchChunks = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(".achievement-glitch");

  if (glitchChunks.length) {
    if (prefersReducedMotion) {
      // Show plainly, no glitch/texture/proximity-gating — see the
      // component-level comment for why this shows the chunk instead
      // of just leaving it hidden.
      for (const chunk of glitchChunks) {
        chunk.classList.add("is-visible", "no-motion");
      }
    } else {
      const revealRadius = 180; // px, distance from a chunk's center that reveals it
      const hideDelay = 2500; // ms, how long a chunk lingers after the cursor leaves
      const glitchIntervalMin = 500; // ms, shortest gap between re-glitches while visible
      const glitchIntervalMax = 1400; // ms, longest gap between re-glitches while visible

      const chunkState = Array.from(glitchChunks).map((el) => ({
        el,
        visible: false,
        hideTimeout: null as ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null,
        glitchTimeout: null as ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null,
      }));

      // Force a reflow before re-adding `.is-visible` so the one-shot
      // `glitch-pop`/`glitch-scan` CSS animations restart from frame 0
      // every time, rather than silently not restarting because the
      // class never really left the "applied" state from the
      // browser's point of view. Used both for the initial reveal and
      // for every re-glitch while a chunk lingers on screen below.
      function playGlitch(el: HTMLElement) {
        el.classList.remove("is-visible");
        void el.offsetWidth;
        el.classList.add("is-visible");
      }

      // While a chunk is visible, keep re-triggering the glitch flash
      // at random intervals — the initial reveal already plays one,
      // this just keeps it looking "unstable" for as long as it
      // lingers, instead of the animation playing once and then
      // sitting there static for the full 2-3s hideDelay. Stops
      // rescheduling itself the moment the chunk is hidden.
      function scheduleGlitch(s: (typeof chunkState)[number]) {
        const delay = glitchIntervalMin + Math.random() * (glitchIntervalMax - glitchIntervalMin);
        s.glitchTimeout = setTimeout(() => {
          if (s.visible) {
            playGlitch(s.el);
            scheduleGlitch(s);
          }
        }, delay);
      }

      window.addEventListener("mousemove", (e) => {
        for (const s of chunkState) {
          const rect = s.el.getBoundingClientRect();
          const cx = rect.left + rect.width / 2;
          const cy = rect.top + rect.height / 2;
          const dist = Math.hypot(e.clientX - cx, e.clientY - cy);
          const shouldShow = dist < revealRadius;

          if (shouldShow) {
            // Cursor's back in range (or never left) — cancel any
            // pending hide so a brief dip out of range doesn't cut the
            // lingering period short.
            if (s.hideTimeout !== null) {
              clearTimeout(s.hideTimeout);
              s.hideTimeout = null;
            }

            if (!s.visible) {
              s.visible = true;
              playGlitch(s.el);
              scheduleGlitch(s);
            }
          } else if (s.visible && s.hideTimeout === null) {
            // Cursor just left range — don't hide immediately, let it
            // linger for `hideDelay` first. If the cursor comes back
            // before that fires, the branch above cancels it.
            s.hideTimeout = setTimeout(() => {
              s.visible = false;
              s.hideTimeout = null;
              if (s.glitchTimeout !== null) {
                clearTimeout(s.glitchTimeout);
                s.glitchTimeout = null;
              }
              s.el.classList.remove("is-visible");
            }, hideDelay);
          }
        }
      });
    }
  }
</script>

<style>
  .achievement-section {
    position: relative;
    min-height: 100vh;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    padding: clamp(3rem, 8vw, 7rem) clamp(1.5rem, 6vw, 6rem);
    /* No `overflow: hidden` here — see the comment on `overflow-x:
       hidden` on `html` in Layout.astro. That handles horizontal
       clipping page-wide, so an island that needs more vertical room
       than this section provides isn't hard-clipped. */
    background-size: cover;
    background-position: top;
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
    /* Seam-blend targets (see the shared comment in Layout.astro) —
       one variable per edge since this section needs two different
       colors. Top softens into var(--sky-3)
       (EducationIslandSection's bottom); bottom softens into
       var(--dusk-blue) (BonusIslandSection's top). */
    --seam-target-top: var(--sky-3);
    --seam-target-bottom: var(--dusk-blue);
  }

  .content {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 2;

    width: min(32ch, 100%);
    margin: 0 auto;

    text-align: left;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #555;
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    color: #1a1a1a;
  }

  .body-text {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: #444;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .achievement-island {
    position: absolute;
    z-index: 1;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .achievement-island-inner img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  /* Statue island: narrower and taller than a typical flanking island,
     mid-left but a bit further in from the edge than
     ComputerIslandSection's telephone-pole island was. Doesn't bleed
     off any edge. */
  .achievement-island-left {
    top: 30vh;
    left: 22vw;
    width: clamp(130px, 12vw, 200px);
    animation: bob-left 6.5s ease-in-out infinite;
  }

  /* Temple island: large, sitting lower and tucked in much closer to
     the right edge than the computer island was. Also doesn't bleed
     off any edge. */
  .achievement-island-right {
    top: 16vh;
    right: 2vw;
    width: clamp(320px, 40vw, 560px);
    animation: bob-right 5.8s ease-in-out infinite;
  }

  @keyframes bob-left {
    0%, 100% { transform: translateY(0); }
    50% { transform: translateY(-10px); }
  }

  @keyframes bob-right {
    0%, 100% { transform: translateY(0); }
    50% { transform: translateY(-8px); }
  }

  /* Front rock chunks: same `position: absolute; z-index: 1;
     pointer-events: none;` treatment as `.achievement-island` (see
     the component-level comment above for why z-index 1 specifically
     matters — it's what keeps these excluded from the halftone
     spotlight's blend in Layout.astro, same as every other island).
     `opacity: 0` by default — JS toggles `.is-visible` on/off based
     on cursor proximity per chunk (see the <script> above); nothing
     here shows them permanently. The bob animation still runs continuously
     underneath regardless of visibility, so a chunk is never caught
     mid-drift when it pops back in. */
  .achievement-glitch {
    position: absolute;
    z-index: 1;
    pointer-events: none;
    opacity: 0;
  }

  .achievement-glitch-inner img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  /* Chunk 1 (bigger, closer to the temple): tucked just under-left of
     the temple island's base. Eyeballed off your reference screenshot
     — nudge once it's live. */
  .achievement-glitch-1 {
    top: 70vh;
    right: 24vw;
    width: clamp(140px, 20vw, 300px);
    animation: bob-chunk-1 6.2s ease-in-out infinite;
  }

  /* Chunk 2 (smaller, further down-left): sits lower and further left
     than chunk 1, partly below it, like it broke off and drifted
     further from the main island. */
  .achievement-glitch-2 {
    top: 81vh;
    right: 34vw;
    width: clamp(110px, 17vw, 260px);
    animation: bob-chunk-2 7s ease-in-out infinite;
  }

  @keyframes bob-chunk-1 {
    0%, 100% { transform: translateY(0); }
    50% { transform: translateY(-9px); }
  }

  @keyframes bob-chunk-2 {
    0%, 100% { transform: translateY(0); }
    50% { transform: translateY(-7px); }
  }

  /* One-shot "digital tear" that plays whenever `.is-visible` is
     freshly (re)applied by the script above: `animation-fill-mode:
     both` holds the opacity-0 -> 1 handoff cleanly at both ends (no
     flash of the un-animated state before frame 0, no snap-back after
     the last frame), and `steps(1, jump-end)` makes the browser HOLD
     each keyframe's values for its whole segment and then jump
     straight to the next, rather than smoothly tweening `clip-path`/
     `filter` between them — that abrupt jump-cut is what actually
     reads as "glitchy" instead of "fading in oddly." */
  .achievement-glitch.is-visible {
    opacity: 1;
    animation: glitch-pop 0.45s steps(1, jump-end) both;
  }

  @keyframes glitch-pop {
    0% { clip-path: none; filter: none; }
    12% { clip-path: inset(8% 0 46% 0); filter: hue-rotate(25deg) saturate(2.2); }
    24% { clip-path: inset(58% 0 4% 0); filter: hue-rotate(-20deg) saturate(1.6); }
    38% { clip-path: inset(0 18% 0 0); filter: none; }
    52% { clip-path: inset(32% 0 28% 0); filter: hue-rotate(12deg); }
    68% { clip-path: inset(0 0 0 10%); filter: none; }
    84% { clip-path: inset(4% 0 4% 0); filter: hue-rotate(-8deg); }
    /* `none` here, not `inset(0 0 0 0)` — the two look identical while
       the chunk is sitting still, but `inset(0 0 0 0)` still
       establishes a real clip region exactly at this element's own
       box. `animation-fill-mode: both` locks whatever this 100%
       keyframe says for as long as `.is-visible` stays applied (most
       of the 1.4-2.8s a chunk is on screen) — with an actual inset
       value there, that lingering clip region was cropping the inner
       image the moment the mouse-repel drift (a `transform` on the
       CHILD `.achievement-glitch-inner`) pushed it visually past this
       element's own edges, which is exactly the "looks like it's
       trapped in a box" cut-off. `none` removes the clip region
       entirely instead of just visually matching "no clip" at rest,
       so the drifting image is free to render past this box's edges
       same as every other island's does. */
    100% { clip-path: none; filter: none; }
  }

  /* Scanline/static texture — plain CSS, no image asset. Only present
     while a chunk is visible (`.is-visible::after`), faded in via its
     own one-shot `glitch-scan` animation on the same `steps()` snap
     timing as `glitch-pop`, so the texture itself flickers in rather
     than smoothly fading over the image. `mix-blend-mode: overlay`
     lets it read as a texture ON the chunk rather than a flat
     translucent sheet sitting on top of it. */
  .achievement-glitch::after {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background: repeating-linear-gradient(
      to bottom,
      rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.4) 0px,
      rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.4) 1px,
      transparent 1px,
      transparent 3px
    );
    mix-blend-mode: overlay;
    opacity: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .achievement-glitch.is-visible::after {
    animation: glitch-scan 0.45s steps(1, jump-end) both;
  }

  @keyframes glitch-scan {
    0% { opacity: 0; }
    12% { opacity: 0.9; }
    24% { opacity: 0.2; }
    38% { opacity: 0.8; }
    52% { opacity: 0.3; }
    68% { opacity: 0.7; }
    100% { opacity: 0.45; }
  }

  /* Reduced motion: shown plainly, no flicker/glitch/texture — see
     the component-level comment above for why this shows the chunk
     rather than hiding it outright. The `.no-motion` class is only
     ever added by the script above, and only when
     `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` is set, so this rule can't
     accidentally fire for anyone else. */
  .achievement-glitch.no-motion {
    animation: none;
  }

  .achievement-glitch.no-motion::after {
    display: none;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .achievement-island-left,
    .achievement-island-right,
    .achievement-glitch-1,
    .achievement-glitch-2 {
      animation: none;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .content {
      width: min(32ch, 100%);
      padding-inline: 1.5rem;
    }

    /* Same fix as ComputerIslandSection: the mid-left absolute
       position sits right under the (now full-width) text on mobile,
       so drop it into normal document flow below the content instead
       of overlaying it. `order` moves it after `.content` visually
       even though it's first in the DOM; `.achievement-section`'s
       existing `place-items: center` centers it automatically once
       it's back in flow. Margin-top is the knob for "how far below
       the text it sits" — smaller value pulls it up closer to the
